{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/gitstore at exact clean pushed head b91d6a7283ced2525be41196f32edc10d3634d75 (branch request/railway-record-framing, on origin), answering child request 9f0ad770 under promise e34a8281, after codex's ratified changes-requested ca9953ac. Replaces artifact f42cd5c0 at head 2520cffd.\n\nThe blocking finding was that my load-bearing claim was too broad, and codex was right. I reproduced it independently before repairing rather than accepting the report: a raw commit object written with git hash-object --literally, body carrying a NUL, reachable through a ref. git fsck --strict calls it nulInCommit, and git log -z still emitted eight valid-looking fields with the body reading SECRET - TRUNCATED-AFTER-THIS silently gone. Graph succeeded and dropped attacker-controlled metadata. Neither round-trip nor fail closed.\n\nHow the claim was wrong, precisely, because it is the more useful half. I tested the invariant through git commit, saw it refuse a NUL, and generalised from the door an honest committer uses to the whole object store. hash-object --literally is the plumbing path and objects arriving by push never touch porcelain at all. The porcelain guarantee covers objects git wrote, not objects git merely stores, and the code comment now says exactly that rather than resting on the earlier over-claim.\n\nThe repair is the smallest thing that closes it, per condition 4: one batched git cat-file --batch over the hashes already returned, refusing if any raw object carries the byte the framing depends on. No second delimiter protocol, no per-commit subprocess. Removing that guard fails the new regression, so it is load-bearing.\n\nThe regression builds the object with plumbing on purpose and reaches it through update-ref rather than through anything the renderer chose. A test that only creates commits the way an honest committer does cannot reach this case, which is exactly how the gap survived the first review.\n\nHostile decorations are now settled by test rather than by claim, per condition 3. Git refuses \\x1f, \\x1e, \\x07 and \\x00 in ref names, so decorations are covered by git's ref-name validation and not by this parser. My earlier statement that the six hostile cases covered decorations was wrong: every one of them plants bytes in a commit message. The test exists so a future git that relaxed the rule fails here rather than quietly widening exposure.\n\nUnchanged and confirmed by codex in the previous round: the fixed-stride NUL framing that closes the 0x1e/0x1f forgery, the field-count and timestamp refusals, the split parser, and the rule that only the trailing -z token is dropped while an empty middle field is kept.\n\nGates at this exact head: go test ./... 29 packages ok, go vet clean, make docs clean, gofmt clean, git diff --check clean, worktree clean. The branch is pushed to origin, which codex noted was missing.\n\n2520cffd is not merged and artifact f42cd5c0 is retired.","body":{"commit":"b91d6a7283ced2525be41196f32edc10d3634d75","path":"internal/gitstore"}}